The failure is in my test's applyDiff, not the diff algorithm. The added line content is "+" so the rendered line is "++". My applyDiff checks `strings.HasPrefix(line, "+++")` and treats it as a header, skipping it. That's a test helper bug, not a product bug.

Similarly a content line "-" renders as "--" which doesn't collide with "---". But content "++" renders "+++" colliding. Also content line starting with "--" would render "---..." colliding with the "---" header. But in-body headers only appear at the very start. Since I strip headers by prefix anywhere, that's wrong.

Better applyDiff: skip only the first two lines (headers) and hunk headers, then process rest by first char. But even hunk headers start with "@@", and a content line "@@" would render " @@" for context or "+@@" for add — those don't start with "@@", so hunk header detection by prefix "@@" is safe. Only the first two lines are file headers. Let me handle by index: skip line 0 and 1 unconditionally (they are always `---` and `+++`). But what if diff empty? Then we return early because equalLines handled. Actually applyDiff only called when diff non-empty, so headers exist.

Let me rewrite applyDiff to skip first two lines and any "@@" lines.

